// Written exam practice. model: true = model answer from the revision pack; false = suggested answer written from the pack notes.
window.WRITTEN = [
  { q: 'What is the correct order of structural organisation in the human body, from smallest to largest?', marks: 1, model: true, a: 'Cell → Tissue → Organ → Organ System. Cells are the smallest living units; similar cells form tissues; multiple tissues form organs; organs group into systems to carry out major body functions.' },
  { q: 'Describe TWO structural features of the alveolus that make it adapted for efficient gas exchange.', marks: 2, model: true, a: '1. Thin walls — alveolar walls are one cell thick (simple squamous epithelium), minimising the diffusion distance for O₂ and CO₂.\n2. Rich blood supply — surrounding pulmonary capillaries (also one cell thick) maintain a steep concentration gradient.\n(Also accept: large total surface area ~70 m²; moist lining dissolves gases.)' },
  { q: 'Explain the role of the myelin sheath in the nervous system.', marks: 2, model: true, a: 'The myelin sheath is a fatty (lipid) insulating layer wrapped around the axons of neurons by Schwann cells (in the PNS). It speeds up nerve impulse transmission through saltatory conduction: the impulse jumps between the gaps in myelin called nodes of Ranvier, rather than travelling along the entire axon. This increases the speed of conduction and reduces energy expenditure.' },
  { q: 'State where bile is produced, stored, and released. What is its role in digestion?', marks: 3, model: true, a: 'Bile is PRODUCED by hepatocytes in the LIVER. It is STORED and concentrated in the GALLBLADDER. It is RELEASED into the duodenum via the common bile duct when fatty food is detected. Its role: bile EMULSIFIES fats — it breaks large fat globules into many small droplets, greatly increasing the surface area available for pancreatic lipase to digest triglycerides into fatty acids and glycerol. Bile does not chemically digest fats.' },
  { q: 'Name the TWO primary lymphoid organs and ONE secondary lymphoid organ, and give ONE function of each.', marks: 3, model: true, a: 'Primary lymphoid organs (where lymphocytes are produced and mature):\n1. Thymus — site where T-lymphocytes mature and become immunocompetent; essential for cell-mediated immunity.\n2. Bone marrow — site of haematopoiesis; where B-lymphocytes are produced and mature.\nSecondary lymphoid organ (where mature lymphocytes meet antigens and immune responses are mounted):\n3. Spleen — filters blood, removes old red blood cells, houses lymphocytes and mounts immune responses against blood-borne pathogens. (Also accept: lymph nodes — filter lymph; tonsils; Peyer\'s patches.)' },
  { q: 'Describe the path of a red blood cell from the right ventricle to the left atrium. What happens in the lungs?', marks: 3, model: true, a: 'Right ventricle → pulmonary artery (pulmonary trunk) → pulmonary capillaries surrounding the alveoli. In the lungs: CO₂ diffuses from blood into alveolar air; O₂ diffuses from alveolar air into blood (simple diffusion down concentration gradients); O₂ binds to haemoglobin to form oxyhaemoglobin → pulmonary veins (×4) → left atrium. Pulmonary veins are the only veins carrying oxygenated blood.' },
  { q: 'Explain how the layered structure of the skin supports its BARRIER function. Refer to at least TWO layers.', marks: 4, model: true, a: 'Epidermis: outermost dead, keratinised cells filled with keratin form a waterproof layer that prevents water loss and blocks most pathogens, chemicals and UV. Melanocytes produce melanin, which absorbs UV.\nDermis: sebaceous glands produce sebum, which lubricates the surface and has mild antimicrobial properties. Langerhans cells (lower epidermis) detect foreign antigens.\nHypodermis: subcutaneous fat cushions the body from mechanical injury.' },
  { q: 'Identify the correct body system for each structure: Loop of Henle / Myelin sheath / Fimbriae / Cerebellum.', marks: 4, model: true, a: 'Loop of Henle → urinary (renal) system — part of the nephron.\nMyelin sheath → nervous system — insulates axons.\nFimbriae → female reproductive system — finger-like projections at the end of the fallopian tube.\nCerebellum → nervous system (CNS) — coordinates balance and movement.' },
  { q: 'Describe the path of an oxygen molecule from the atmosphere to a red blood cell in a pulmonary capillary.', hint: 'Start at the nose/mouth. End at haemoglobin inside the RBC.', marks: 4, model: false, a: 'Nose/mouth (nasal cavity warms, filters and humidifies air) → pharynx → larynx → trachea (held open by C-shaped cartilage) → bronchus → bronchioles → alveolus. O₂ dissolves in the moist alveolar lining, diffuses across the one-cell-thick alveolar wall and capillary wall into the blood, enters the red blood cell and binds to haemoglobin to form oxyhaemoglobin.' },
  { q: 'Explain why the left ventricle has a thicker wall than the right ventricle.', hint: 'Think about where each side pumps blood to and the pressure required.', marks: 3, model: false, a: 'The left ventricle pumps blood through the aorta to the whole body (systemic circulation), which needs much higher pressure. The right ventricle only pumps blood a short distance to the lungs (pulmonary circulation) at lower pressure, which also protects the delicate lung capillaries. So the left ventricle needs more cardiac muscle.' },
  { q: 'What is the functional significance of villi and microvilli in the small intestine?', hint: 'Surface area, absorption, structures within the villus.', marks: 3, model: false, a: 'Villi and microvilli greatly increase the surface area for absorption. Each villus has a capillary network that absorbs glucose and amino acids into the blood (→ hepatic portal vein), and a lacteal that absorbs fats as chylomicrons into the lymph. Thin walls give a short diffusion distance.' },
  { q: 'Describe THREE functions of the skin.', hint: 'Think: protection, thermoregulation, sensation, vitamin D, excretion.', marks: 3, model: false, a: '1. Protection — keratinised epidermis forms a waterproof barrier against water loss, pathogens and UV (melanin).\n2. Thermoregulation — sweat evaporates to cool the body; arrector pili raise hairs to trap air; dermal blood vessels regulate heat loss.\n3. Sensation — receptors such as Meissner\'s corpuscles (light touch), Pacinian corpuscles (pressure) and free nerve endings (pain, temperature).\n(Also: vitamin D production, excretion of salts in sweat.)' },
  { q: 'Trace the path of bile from production to its role in the duodenum.', hint: 'Liver → ? → ? → duodenum. What does bile do when it arrives?', marks: 3, model: false, a: 'Produced by hepatocytes in the liver → hepatic duct → stored and concentrated in the gallbladder (via the cystic duct) → released through the cystic duct into the common bile duct → sphincter of Oddi → duodenum. There it emulsifies fats into small droplets, increasing the surface area for pancreatic lipase.' },
  { q: 'What structural features of neurons allow rapid, long-distance signal transmission?', hint: 'Consider axon length, myelin, nodes of Ranvier.', marks: 3, model: false, a: 'A long axon carries impulses over long distances. The myelin sheath (made by Schwann cells) insulates the axon. Gaps in the myelin (nodes of Ranvier) let the impulse jump from node to node (saltatory conduction), making transmission much faster.' },
  { q: 'Compare the dermis and epidermis: state ONE structural difference and ONE functional difference.', hint: 'Think about what lives in each layer and what each layer protects or provides.', marks: 2, model: false, a: 'Structural: the epidermis is avascular stratified squamous keratinised epithelium; the dermis is dense irregular connective tissue containing blood vessels, nerves and glands.\nFunctional: the epidermis is a waterproof protective barrier (keratin, melanin); the dermis provides sensation, thermoregulation and nutrients for the epidermis.' },
  { q: 'Describe the role of the thymus in the immune system.', hint: 'Which lymphocyte matures there? Why is this process important?', marks: 3, model: false, a: 'The thymus is a primary lymphoid organ in the mediastinum where T-lymphocytes mature and become immunocompetent (able to recognise antigens). This is essential for cell-mediated immunity. It is large in childhood and shrinks after puberty.' },
  { q: 'Trace the path of a sound wave from the pinna to the generation of a nerve impulse in the cochlea.', hint: 'pinna → canal → eardrum → ossicles → oval window → cochlea → ?', marks: 4, model: false, a: 'The pinna collects sound → external auditory canal → tympanic membrane vibrates → malleus → incus → stapes (amplify vibrations) → stapes pushes the oval window → pressure waves in cochlear fluid → hair cells of the organ of Corti bend → nerve impulse → auditory (vestibulocochlear) nerve → brain.' },
  { q: 'Explain, using named structures, how the digestive and cardiovascular systems work together to deliver absorbed glucose to a muscle cell.', hint: 'Start at the villus capillary. Remember the hepatic portal system. End at the muscle capillary.', marks: 5, model: false, a: 'Glucose is absorbed into the capillaries of the villi in the small intestine → hepatic portal vein → liver (processes/stores glucose) → hepatic vein → inferior vena cava → right atrium → right ventricle → pulmonary artery → lungs → pulmonary veins → left atrium → left ventricle → aorta → systemic arteries → arterioles → muscle capillaries, where glucose diffuses into the muscle cell.' },
];

